<!--
  Status-bar icon: sunrise, the "Horizon" launcher mark redrawn as a
  silhouette. Android alpha-masks small icons, so this is a monochrome shape
  on transparent ground and NOT @mipmap/ic_launcher — see CLAUDE.md.

  The composition is the launcher's, ZOOMED 1.3x so the horizon runs off both
  edges (2026-08-02). Sun-on-a-line is inherently wide and short: at 1x it
  filled 92% of the width but only 35% of the height and read as a small mark
  in a big box, while Nivaat's upright shuttle fills 85%. Uniform scaling was
  capped — the bar already spanned 92% of the width — so the line is drawn
  past the viewport instead (x -2.5 to 26.5, clipped). A horizon that leaves
  the frame is what a horizon does, and it buys a 30% bigger sun: fills 100%
  x 46% now.

  Ratios are unchanged from the launcher-matched version: sun r9.9 against a
  visible line of 24, so the line still clears the sun by 2.1 a side. Do not
  zoom further — at 1.45x that overshoot drops under 1 and the line stops
  reading as a horizon, becoming the sun's baseline.

  Two floors, both device-caught, neither negotiable:

  * Line thickness. The launcher's is 1.46% of canvas; reproduced faithfully
    (~1px on a 3x screen) it was INVISIBLE in the status bar. 0.7 units is the
    proven floor at 1x; this is 1.15.
  * The gap. The launcher's sun sits ON its line. At this size an attached
    line reads as the dome's base — only the stubs either side show — so the
    horizon disappears as surely as if it were too thin.

  Earlier failures worth not repeating: dome 86% of the bar's width with a bar
  30% of its height read as a burger; shrinking the dome to fix it (r5.8) made
  the mark look small, since the dome carries the weight.

  tools/preview_notif_icons.py draws it at 18px, but even 18px was optimistic
  here — the status bar is the only real judge.
-->
